1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,586
Your workout took an hour.

2
00:00:01,736 --> 00:00:03,234
But where did that hour go?

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,433
An hour in the gym is not an hour
of lifting.

4
00:00:06,583 --> 00:00:09,926
In this made-up example: fifteen
minutes lifting.

5
00:00:10,076 --> 00:00:12,584
Twenty-five resting. Eight setting
up.

6
00:00:12,734 --> 00:00:13,791
And twelve waiting.

7
00:00:14,167 --> 00:00:16,315
Those are four different
problems.

8
00:00:16,465 --> 00:00:19,332
Start with a delay you can
actually change.

9
00:00:19,482 --> 00:00:21,479
Like repeated trips for
equipment.

10
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:23,912
A time audit is not a reason

11
00:00:24,062 --> 00:00:26,105
to rush every rest period.

12
00:00:26,255 --> 00:00:29,146
The biggest block is not
automatically the problem.

13
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,217
For one session, log the four
buckets.

14
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:34,073
Make one practical change.

15
00:00:34,223 --> 00:00:35,976
Then compare the next session.

16
00:00:36,126 --> 00:00:38,483
Measure the friction before you
rewrite the plan.

17
00:00:38,867 --> 00:00:40,353
Know why. Train better.
